# Copy this file to .env and fill in the values for YOUR Postgres setup, then:
#   chmod 600 .env
# .env is git-ignored — the real one never gets committed. Every value below has a sane default
# in the notebooks, so you only need to set what's actually different for your machine.
#
# The notebooks connect the same way no matter how Postgres is running (Docker, Postgres.app,
# Homebrew, apt, the EDB installer, ...) — only the values below change. Pick the block that
# matches your setup and delete the other.

# ── Option A: Docker, or any Postgres with a superuser password set ────────────────────────────
# (This is what a fresh `docker run postgres` or the official Docker image gives you by default —
#  user "postgres", whatever password you set when starting the container.)
PGHOST=localhost
PGPORT=5432
PGUSER=postgres
PGPASSWORD=changeme
PGDATABASE=study

# ── Option B: a native local install using peer/trust auth (no password) ───────────────────────
# (Common on a fresh Postgres.app or Homebrew install on macOS, where your OS login is also a
#  Postgres superuser and no password is required for local connections.)
# PGHOST=localhost
# PGPORT=5432
# PGUSER=your_os_username
# PGPASSWORD=
# PGDATABASE=study

# Notes:
#   - PGDATABASE ("study" above) must already exist — the notebooks create the `kyc` schema
#     inside it, but never CREATE DATABASE, since it may be a database you share with others.
#   - Leaving PGPASSWORD blank/unset is fine — the notebooks fall back to peer auth / ~/.pgpass
#     rather than sending an empty password.
#   - If your server is on a different host/port (a remote box, a non-default Docker mapping),
#     set PGHOST / PGPORT accordingly.
